  "prediction": "**Summary of Recent News**\n\n**Montana Congressional Election and Assault Incident**\n\nIn a recent incident, Montana congressman-elect Greg Gianforte pleaded guilty to misdemeanor assault for \"body slamming\" a reporter, Ben Jacobs, during a special election campaign in 2017. Gianforte was sentenced to a 180-day deferred sentence, 40 hours of community service, 20 hours of anger management, and a $300 fine. He has apologized to the journalist and pledged to donate $50,000 to the Committee to Protect Journalists.\n\n**President Trump's Comments on Gianforte's Incident**\n\nPresident Trump has made light of Gianforte's physical assault on a reporter, saying it was a \"tremendous success\" and praising Gianforte as a \"tough cookie.\" Trump's comments have been widely condemned by the White House Correspondents' Association, which stated that the president's praise for a violent assault on a reporter is an attack on the First Amendment.\n\n**Montana Senate Race**\n\nThe Montana Senate race is a closely contested election between incumbent Democratic Senator Jon Tester and Republican challenger Matt Rosendale. President Trump has made it his mission to unseat Tester, who publically released information about alleged misconduct that led to White House physician Adm. Ronny Jackson's failed nomination to lead the Department of Veterans Affairs. Trump has falsely claimed that Democrats are supporting a caravan of immigrants traveling up from Central America.\n\n**Social Media Update**\n\nTwitter has announced that users can add location information to their tweets, including their city or precise location. Users also have the option to delete their tweet location history.\n\n**Key Takeaways**\n\n* Greg Gianforte pleaded guilty to misdemeanor assault for \"body slamming\" a reporter during a special election campaign in 2017.\n* President Trump has made light of Gianforte's physical assault on a reporter, praising him as a \"tough cookie.\"\n* The Montana